Issues.Body:
When an async JavaScript call fails with `AbortError`, a Blazor app may have to inspect `JSException.Message` to distinguish an aborted operation from other failures. This PR proposes a read-only `string? Name` property on `JSException` so callers can catch the error by its JavaScript name:

```csharp
try
{
    await JSRuntime.InvokeVoidAsync("performOperation");
}
catch (JSException ex) when (ex.Name == "AbortError")
{
    // Handle the aborted operation.
}
```

The exception type, existing public constructors, and message text (including the JavaScript stack) stay the same. `Name` is `null` when no string name is available. If reading the JavaScript `name` property throws, the caller still receives the original formatted error. The change is limited to asynchronous .NET-to-JavaScript calls; synchronous in-process calls are unchanged.

**Compatibility.** The updated .NET reader accepts both legacy failures (a string or `null`) and the new structured failures. An older .NET reader cannot read a structured failure from newer JavaScript. Before enabling the writer, do we need to support that version pairing for the standalone interop package or custom dispatchers?

**API review.** The proposed API adds only `JSException.Name`; it does not introduce exception subclasses. The companion API proposal is drafted. Would a maintainer be willing to champion it?

**Validation.** All 52 focused JavaScript tests and 193 managed JSInterop tests passed. The named-error browser test passed on both Blazor Server and WebAssembly. The JSInterop package, Web.JS Debug and Release builds, and the browser test project built successfully. The full repository suite and mixed JavaScript/.NET version pairings were not tested.

Related to #53620.

## Background and Motivation

Originating task: [dotnet/aspnetcore#69884](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/issues/69884).

Owner and API review champion: @MayaKirova.

A [send-only prototype](https://github.com/MayaKirova/aspnetcore/commit/3ae43442a2d973d2a6999205f248ca1fac2b068f) is available on [MayaKirova/aspnetcore, `mkirova/browser-messaging`](https://github.com/MayaKirova/aspnetcore/tree/mkirova/browser-messaging). It implements construction, posting, start/close operations, and disposal, including all three posting overloads below. It does **not** implement event registration or the proposed `MessageEvent` snapshot. No implementation pull request is linked yet.

Event support follows [dotnet/aspnetcore#69922](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/issues/69922), a prerequisite design rather than a contract approved by this proposal. This proposal requests review of the complete send-and-receive shape; the prototype is evidence for the send portion only.

The originating issue requests browser messaging primitives for communication between windows, tabs, and worker-related contexts. This proposal adds typed access to the browser's `MessageChannel`, `MessagePort`, and `BroadcastChannel` APIs through `IBrowserPlatform.Window`.

This proposes a **send-and-receive, JSON-only slice**, not the complete scope of the originating issue:

- Create independent message channels and named broadcast channels.
- Obtain the two stable ports owned by a message channel.
- Post JSON values, start a port, and close a port or broadcast channel.
- React to `message` and `messageerror` through typed, independently removable listeners.
- Dispose caller-owned channels and release their resources.
- Defer transfer lists and non-JSON structured-clone values.

Receiving messages depends on the event design in [dotnet/aspnetcore#69922](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/issues/69922). The messaging proposal includes those consumers now, but implementation must wait for the accepted shared design and its merged infrastructure. It does not propose a competing callback bridge, C# `event`, or `SetOnMessageAsync` slot.

The useful first scenarios are a round trip between the two ports of a channel and receiving notifications from other channel instances with the same broadcast name, including instances in other eligible tabs. A send-only surface is not the intended shipping milestone. Port transfer to a worker or another window remains outside this slice.

Posting supports a direct `string` convenience overload, a per-call `PostMessageAsync<T>(T message, JsonTypeInfo<T> jsonTypeInfo)` serialization overload, and a low-level `JsonElement` overload. The generic overload sends the JSON representation defined by the supplied contract, not an arbitrary CLR object or its CLR type identity. No application-wide serializer configuration or reflection fallback is proposed.

This is **not** an established package-wide mapping for arbitrary Web IDL `any`. The [Fetch proposal, dotnet/aspnetcore#70046](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/issues/70046) uses `JsonElement` for the necessarily-JSON result of `json()` but defers arbitrary `cancel(any reason)` and `abort(any reason)` inputs. Messaging's serialization policy is a separate proposed decision. JSON objects, arrays, strings, numbers, booleans, and null are in scope; non-finite native numbers raise the receive-side question noted below.

The proposed `MessageEvent.Data` applies the same JSON-only boundary to received messages. It is an owned snapshot, usable after the callback returns, not a live JavaScript object. Arbitrary native senders can still produce non-JSON payloads; their treatment is an explicit design question below, not something the existing event prototype already solves.

### Standards and scope inventory

Sources: [Webref HTML IDL](https://github.com/w3c/webref/blob/main/ed/idl/html.idl) and the [HTML messaging specification](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/web-messaging.html).

| Native interface or member | Decision | Reason |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `MessageChannel` constructor | Implement | `Window.MessageChannel.CreateAsync()` creates a caller-owned channel. |
| `MessageChannel.port1`, `port2` | Implement | Stable, non-nullable `MessagePort` properties, owned by their channel. |
| `MessagePort.postMessage(any message)` with omitted options | Project limited value subset | Direct string overload, per-call JSON metadata for CLR messages, and a low-level `JsonElement` overload; no transfers. |
| `MessagePort.postMessage(message, sequence<object> transfer)` | Defer | Transferable-object identity and ownership need a separate design. |
| `MessagePort.postMessage(message, StructuredSerializeOptions options)` | Defer explicit options | Omitting native options is supported; an explicit options overload is not exposed yet. |
| `MessagePort.start()`, `close()` | Implement | `StartAsync()` and `CloseAsync()`, preserving native behavior. |
| `MessageEventTarget.onmessage`, `onmessageerror`, included by `MessagePort` | Project events through shared pattern | `MessagePort.MessageEvent` and `MessageErrorEvent` typed keys; no single-handler property. |
| `BroadcastChannel` constructor and `name` | Implement | `CreateAsync(string)` and `GetNameAsync()`. |
| `BroadcastChannel.postMessage(any message)` | Implement JSON subset | Same explicit JSON-only input contract as message ports. |
| `BroadcastChannel.close()` | Implement | `CloseAsync()`. |
| `BroadcastChannel.onmessage`, `onmessageerror` | Project events through shared pattern | `BroadcastChannel.MessageEvent` and `MessageErrorEvent` typed keys. |
| Inherited `EventTarget.addEventListener` and removal | Project shared subset | Two `AddEventListenerAsync` overloads per target, independent disposable registrations, and cancellation-based removal as proposed in dotnet/aspnetcore#69922. |
| `EventTarget.dispatchEvent` and `AddEventListenerOptions.once`/`passive` | Defer | No synthetic dispatch API or new listener options beyond the shared proposal. |
| `StructuredSerializeOptions`, including `transfer = []` | Defer | No transfer-list overloads or empty placeholder options type. |
| `MessageEvent.data`, `origin` | Project JSON snapshot | `MessageEvent.Data` and `Origin`, shared by both target-specific event keys. |
| `MessageEvent.lastEventId` | Defer | No event-stream last-event ID is produced by these channel APIs; revisit with EventSource. |
| `MessageEvent.source` | Defer | No sender-object projection for these channel events; no WindowProxy/ServiceWorker source union is introduced. |
| `MessageEvent.ports` | Defer | Requires receiver-owned transferred-port wrappers and identity/ownership rules, not an empty placeholder collection. |
| Native `MessageEvent` constructor, `initMessageEvent()` and inherited `Event` members | Defer | The proposed .NET class is a data snapshot, not an event-construction, propagation, or dispatch API. |
| `MessageEventInit`: `data`, `origin`, `lastEventId`, `source`, `ports`, and inherited `EventInit` members | Defer | Used only by the deferred event constructor. |
| `MessageEventSource` typedef: `WindowProxy`, `MessagePort`, or `ServiceWorker` | Defer | No source-union representation is introduced. |
| Worker and AudioWorklet entry points | Exclude from this slice | Native channels are exposed in Window/Worker, and ports additionally in AudioWorklet; this package entry point is `Window`, not a worker or worklet global. |

No enums are needed by this slice. `EventType<TTarget, TEvent>` belongs to the prerequisite proposal, not a second definition in this family.

### Browser support

The operations and proposed events are non-experimental in MDN browser compatibility data:

| Implemented native members | Chrome | Firefox | Safari |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `MessageChannel()` and `port1`/`port2`; `MessagePort.postMessage`, `start`, `close` | 2 | 41 | 5 |
| `BroadcastChannel()`, `name`, `postMessage`, `close` | 54 | 38 | 15.4 |
| `MessagePort.message` | 2 | 41 | 5 |
| `MessagePort.messageerror` | 60 | 57 | 16.4 |
| `BroadcastChannel.message` | 54 | 38 | 15.4 |
| `BroadcastChannel.messageerror` | 60 | 57 | 15.4 |

Sources: [MessageChannel BCD](https://github.com/mdn/browser-compat-data/blob/main/api/MessageChannel.json), [MessagePort BCD](https://github.com/mdn/browser-compat-data/blob/main/api/MessagePort.json), and [BroadcastChannel BCD](https://github.com/mdn/browser-compat-data/blob/main/api/BroadcastChannel.json). Web Platform Status identifies both [channel messaging](https://api.webstatus.dev/v1/features/channel-messaging) and [BroadcastChannel](https://api.webstatus.dev/v1/features/broadcast-channel) as Baseline widely available. These dates describe native browser support, not implementation status in this branch.

## Usage Examples

Register the browser platform in the applicable host and client service collections:

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Platform;
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;

builder.Services.AddBrowserPlatform();
```

In a component, import the projection and inject the browser platform:

```razor
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Platform
@inject IBrowserPlatform Browser
```

The three posting overloads are implemented in the linked prototype. All event examples below describe the **proposed receive API**, which is not implemented by that prototype.

### Posting primitives, classes, and collections

Strings do not require serialization metadata:

```csharp
await channel.PostMessageAsync("ping");
```

Other primitives, classes, and collections use per-call metadata or existing JSON. The application declares a source-generation context; .NET generates its contract properties:

```csharp
using System.Text.Json.Serialization;

public sealed class UpdateMessage
{
    public required string Kind { get; init; }
    public int Version { get; init; }
}

[JsonSourceGenerationOptions(PropertyNamingPolicy = JsonKnownNamingPolicy.CamelCase)]
[JsonSerializable(typeof(bool))]
[JsonSerializable(typeof(int))]
[JsonSerializable(typeof(UpdateMessage))]
[JsonSerializable(typeof(List<UpdateMessage>))]
internal partial class MessagingJsonContext : JsonSerializerContext
{
}
```

```csharp
await channel.PostMessageAsync(true, MessagingJsonContext.Default.Boolean);
await channel.PostMessageAsync(42, MessagingJsonContext.Default.Int32);

await channel.PostMessageAsync(
    new UpdateMessage { Kind = "refresh", Version = 2 },
    MessagingJsonContext.Default.UpdateMessage);

await channel.PostMessageAsync(
    new List<UpdateMessage> { new() { Kind = "refresh", Version = 2 } },
    MessagingJsonContext.Default.ListUpdateMessage);
```

There is no unrestricted `PostMessageAsync<T>(T message)` overload. Collections of primitives also use metadata for their complete collection type. Callers with existing JSON may continue using `PostMessageAsync(JsonElement)`.

### MessageChannel: receive and reply on the connected port

This example uses both ports through .NET. It registers listeners before enabling native dispatch, sends a request, and awaits the reply. The `browser` parameter is the injected `IBrowserPlatform`; `lifetime` is a caller-provided cancellation token.

```csharp
using System.Text.Json;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Platform;

static async Task<string> RoundTripAsync(IBrowserPlatform browser, CancellationToken lifetime)
{
    await using var channel = await browser.Window.MessageChannel.CreateAsync();
    var reply = new TaskCompletionSource<string>(TaskCreationOptions.RunContinuationsAsynchronously);

    await using var receiveReply = await channel.Port1.AddEventListenerAsync(
        MessagePort.MessageEvent, message =>
        {
            reply.TrySetResult(message.Data.GetString()!);
            return Task.CompletedTask;
        }, lifetime);

    await using var receiveRequest = await channel.Port2.AddEventListenerAsync(
        MessagePort.MessageEvent, async message =>
        {
            if (message.Data.GetString() is "ping")
            {
                await channel.Port2.PostMessageAsync("pong");
            }
        }, lifetime);

    await channel.Port1.StartAsync();
    await channel.Port2.StartAsync();
    await channel.Port1.PostMessageAsync("ping");

    return await reply.Task.WaitAsync(lifetime);
}
```

`AddEventListenerAsync` follows native `addEventListener`, not assignment to `onmessage`. It does **not** implicitly start a `MessagePort`. `StartAsync()` enables its queued message dispatch. No transfer or worker API is needed for this local round trip.

### BroadcastChannel: react to updates from another tab

Each participating tab creates its own channel with the same name and installs a listener. The sender's channel instance does not receive its own post; other eligible same-name instances do. This helper can run under a component-owned lifetime, with `onRefresh` updating the component and requesting a render.

```csharp
using System.Text.Json;

static async Task ListenForUpdatesAsync(
    IBrowserPlatform browser,
    Func<Task> onRefresh,
    CancellationToken lifetime)
{
    await using var channel = await browser.Window.BroadcastChannel.CreateAsync("updates");
    await using var messages = await channel.AddEventListenerAsync(
        BroadcastChannel.MessageEvent, async message =>
        {
            var update = message.Data.Deserialize(
                MessagingJsonContext.Default.UpdateMessage)
                ?? throw new JsonException("Expected an update message.");

            if (update.Kind is "refresh")
            {
                await onRefresh();
            }
        }, lifetime);

    await Task.Delay(Timeout.Infinite, lifetime);
}
```

The receiver explicitly selects the same generated contract used for sending, giving it typed property access without a new event overload. The sender's CLR type is not transmitted; incompatible payloads can fail deserialization, and JSON null is rejected explicitly in this example.

An action in another tab, or on another channel instance in the same page, announces an update:

```csharp
await using var sender = await Browser.Window.BroadcastChannel.CreateAsync("updates");
await sender.PostMessageAsync(
    new UpdateMessage { Kind = "refresh", Version = 2 },
    MessagingJsonContext.Default.UpdateMessage);
```

The listening helper exits with cancellation when `lifetime` is canceled; its `await using` scopes then remove the registration and dispose the channel. A component should start listening only after interactivity, cancel its lifetime on disposal, and observe the listening task. Callbacks do not automatically render; the component calls `StateHasChanged` as in the shared event proposal.

### Native message deserialization failure

Both targets also expose a `MessageErrorEvent` key:

```csharp
await using var errors = await channel.AddEventListenerAsync(
    BroadcastChannel.MessageErrorEvent, message =>
    {
        logger.LogError("A broadcast message could not be deserialized. Origin: {Origin}", message.Origin);
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }, lifetime);
```

For a port, use `MessagePort.MessageErrorEvent` with the same registration pattern. `messageerror` reports native receiver deserialization failure; it is not an exception payload and carries no proposed `Exception` or error-message property. Posting failures still propagate from `PostMessageAsync`. A .NET listener exception, or rejection by the proposed JSON projection, is not a native `messageerror`.

The caller disposes each created channel. `Port1` and `Port2` are stable wrappers owned by their channel and should not be disposed separately. `CloseAsync()` performs the native close operation, while `DisposeAsync()` also releases owned resources. Completion of a post means posting completed, not that any recipient processed the message.

Listener handles have their own lifetime. Dispose them before their target, as the examples do. Target disposal must also remove registrations left on that target, and parent-channel disposal must remove registrations on both ports. These target-owned cleanup requirements extend the shared-object event proposal to caller-owned objects and are not implemented yet.

