## Background and Motivation

<!--
We welcome API proposals! We have a process to evaluate the value and shape of new API. There is an overview of our process [here](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/main/docs/APIReviewProcess.md). This template will help us gather the information we need to start the review process.
First, please describe the purpose and value of the new API here.
-->

**Revised proposal: cancellation parameter values, not cancellable interop invocations.** This replaces the original proposal in this issue.

Specification: #70326. Implementation: draft #70328, commit [ec33043d48](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/commit/ec33043d48f74bf5136b8d19e59f0030cf37f4ca). The user clarified that a JSInvokable signature should accept CancellationToken and JavaScript should supply an AbortSignal projection, with the opposite mapping from a .NET token to a native JS signal. Both use JSObjectReference/DotNetObjectReference rather than ordinary JSON state.

The consumer owns a reference lifetime, not a call lifetime. Ordinary invocation results/errors are unchanged. A receiver can retain a value after returning, reuse it in another call, ignore cancellation, or catch cancellation and return normally. The existing InvokeAsync control token remains separate. These are useful end-app and library scenarios for SDK/I/O methods and native browser APIs in Interactive Server and WebAssembly; SSR/prerender have no JS interop.

The outgoing factory is asynchronous to establish the cross-runtime references explicitly instead of initiating unobservable asynchronous setup inside a synchronous JSON converter. A raw outgoing CancellationToken has no disposable ownership surface, so it requires an explicit reference owner.

Status: experimental, not API-approved. Proposed champion remains @javiercn as issue owner; meeting participation is unconfirmed. This issue is not being marked api-ready-for-review.

## Proposed API

<!--
Please provide the specific public API signature diff that you are proposing. For example:
```diff
namespace Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http;

public static class HttpResponseWritingExtensions
{
+    public Task WriteAsync(this HttpResponse response, StringBuilder builder);
}
```
You may find the [Framework Design Guidelines](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/master/docs/coding-guidelines/framework-design-guidelines-digest.md) helpful.
-->

Complete new public .NET surface against main, also recorded in PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt:

```diff
 namespace Microsoft.JSInterop
 {
+    public sealed class DotNetCancellationTokenReference : System.IAsyncDisposable
+    {
+        public static System.Threading.Tasks.ValueTask<DotNetCancellationTokenReference> CreateAsync(
+            IJSRuntime jsRuntime, System.Threading.CancellationToken cancellationToken);
+        public System.Threading.Tasks.ValueTask DisposeAsync();
+    }
+
+    public sealed class JSCancellationTokenReference : System.IAsyncDisposable
+    {
+        public System.Threading.CancellationToken Token { get; }
+        [JSInvokable("CreateCancellationTokenReference")]
+        public static DotNetObjectReference<JSCancellationTokenReference> Create(
+            IJSObjectReference signal, bool isAborted);
+        [JSInvokable]
+        public System.Threading.Tasks.Task CancelAsync();
+        [JSInvokable]
+        public System.Threading.Tasks.ValueTask DisposeAsync();
+    }
 }
```

The second type's factory/callback methods implement the JavaScript-created owner; applications normally use the JavaScript factory and declare CancellationToken parameters rather than calling those methods. Their public placement is an explicit API-review question. No constructor is public.

Declaration-equivalent JS/TypeScript additions to the DotNet module exported by `@microsoft/dotnet-js-interop`:

```diff
 declare module '@microsoft/dotnet-js-interop' {
   export namespace DotNet {
+    export interface CancellationTokenReference {
+      disposeAsync(): Promise<void>;
+    }
+    export function createCancellationTokenReference(signal: AbortSignal): Promise<CancellationTokenReference>;
     export class DotNetObject {
+      createCancellationTokenReference(signal: AbortSignal): Promise<CancellationTokenReference>;
     }
+
+    /** @internal Bridge used by DotNetCancellationTokenReference.CreateAsync. */
+    export function createAbortSignal(reference: DotNetObject, isCancellationRequested: boolean): AbortSignal;
+    /** @internal Bridge used by DotNetCancellationTokenReference.DisposeAsync. */
+    export function disposeAbortSignal(signal: AbortSignal): Promise<void>;
   }
 }
```

The object-bound factory selects that object's runtime. The global factory uses the existing unambiguous default dispatcher and throws when no default can be selected. No ICallDispatcher member or invocation method is added or changed. The previous invokeMethodAsyncWithCancellation APIs are removed from the draft, not retained as compatibility paths.

New parameter convention: a normal CancellationToken argument is decoded from the framework's tracked token reference. It is not injected, need not be last, and may be retained after the method returns. A DotNetCancellationTokenReference argument serializes through the ordinary JSObjectReference converter; the JS function receives the native signal, not the owner object.

## Usage Examples

<!--
Please provide code examples that highlight how the proposed API additions are meant to be consumed.
This will help suggest whether the API has the right shape to be functional, performant and useable.
You can use code blocks like this:
```csharp
// some lines of code here
```
-->

JavaScript to .NET, using a non-final token argument:

```javascript
const controller = new AbortController();
const reference = await dotNetObject.createCancellationTokenReference(controller.signal);
try {
    const result = await dotNetObject.invokeMethodAsync("Load", reference, query);
} finally {
    await reference.disposeAsync();
}
```

```csharp
[JSInvokable]
public Task<SearchResult> Load(CancellationToken cancellationToken, string query);
```

The reference may instead remain alive while the .NET receiver retains the token after returning. An already-aborted input still invokes Load with a canceled token. A handler that ignores or handles cancellation can return normally; interop does not reject the promise because the signal aborted.

.NET to JavaScript:

```csharp
await using var reference =
    await DotNetCancellationTokenReference.CreateAsync(jsRuntime, cancellationToken);
await jsRuntime.InvokeVoidAsync("receiveSignal", reference);
```

```javascript
function receiveSignal(signal) {
    return fetch("/data", { signal });
}
```

Keep the owner alive if JavaScript retains the signal after this call returns. Disposing either direction's reference stops observing without canceling the source token, aborting the signal, or disposing caller-owned services/controllers. The outgoing initial canceled state is applied before entering the JS function.

## Alternative Designs

<!--
Were there other options you considered, such as alternative API shapes?
How does this compare to analogous APIs in other ecosystems and libraries?
-->

- The original call-ID-based cancellation protocol, final-token injection, new invocation methods and special promise rejection were the wrong abstraction for the clarified requirement and are removed.
- Implicit asynchronous converter/reviver setup cannot reliably report creation failures or establish an explicit lifetime before invoking the receiver. Async factories make setup/disposal observable.
- Automatically dispose at call completion: prevents retained tokens/signals from working and incorrectly couples value lifetime to invocation lifetime.
- Automatically serialize raw outgoing CancellationToken: allocates a hidden reference with no caller-visible disposal owner. The draft rejects it with explicit factory guidance.
- Global-only JS factory: cannot select a runtime in mixed applications. The object-bound factory uses the same dispatcher as normal instance interop; the global variant remains available for an unambiguous default.
- Application-owned cancellation registries/callbacks remain possible but repeat reusable bridge logic.

## Risks

<!--
Please mention any risks that to your knowledge the API proposal might entail, such as breaking changes, performance regressions, etc.
-->

- Factory/type names, namespace placement, the public framework callback factory and the two internal JS exports need API review. PublicAPI.Unshipped tracks the actual .NET additions but does not confer approval.
- Explicit owners must be disposed. Completion/failure of a normal invocation deliberately does not dispose them. The outgoing observer retains a token registration and ordinary pending interop promise until cancellation/disposal.
- Creation/disposal incur cross-runtime calls; no benchmark is claimed.
- Abort events cannot await transport. Incoming notification failures are logged to the browser console and retained for disposal to observe, rather than rewriting the application invocation's outcome.
- JavaScript reasons are not translated into .NET cancellation exceptions. Cancellation remains cooperative and does not undo work.
- Server and WebAssembly have separate real-browser evidence. WebView, Native AOT/trimming publish, multithreaded WebAssembly, actual simultaneous multi-runtime execution, Auto transitions, reconnect/resume and other browsers remain unverified. The object-bound dispatcher path exists, but mixed-runtime E2E has not been run.
- Normal disconnected-browser cleanup catches JSDisconnectedException; comprehensive disconnect/resume policy still needs validation. No API is approved for RTM.

### Source justifications

```text
Bidirectional ordinary parameter values: "Cancellation is a parameter value, not an invocation mode." — revised #70326 specification and JSInterop README
Explicit ownership: "Owners dispose the references only when the receiving side no longer needs the value." — revised #70326 specification
No forced result: "A handler that ignores its token remains pending until its own work ends and can then complete normally." — revised #70326 specification
Token placement: "The token occupies an ordinary argument position; it need not be last." — implementation README
Native JS value: "JavaScript receives a native AbortSignal, not a JSON token or a promise wrapper." — implementation README
Actual APIs: complete declarations above match PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt, DotNetCancellationTokenReference.cs, JSCancellationTokenReference.cs and Microsoft.JSInterop.ts in [ec33043d48](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/commit/ec33043d48f74bf5136b8d19e59f0030cf37f4ca)
Unchanged control token: "it still cancels only the managed wait and does not become an AbortSignal or stop the JavaScript function." — implementation README and both-runtime InvocationControlTokenDoesNotBecomeSignalOrStopJavaScript test
```

Validation in draft #70328: 182 managed tests (12 new) and 22 browser tests (10 new plus one ordinary-interop control in each runtime) passed, along with JS Debug/Release builds, declaration typecheck, targeted lint and the E2E graph build. Disabled-forwarding negative controls fail the retained-token and native-signal-after-return assertions; pre-canceled controls remain green. Exact commands and runtime exclusions are on the PR/design issue.

